\caption(The actual native trace supplies all three diagram rows, including the negative first step. Zero endpoints are imposed rather than sampled from the callback.)
\alttext(Three rows show a single interior value one, minus zero point six, then zero point three six, between fixed zero endpoints. Vertical arrows connect consecutive interior states.)

At τ=1/4, r=1/2 instead: the old-time center coefficient is zero, so this
mode is annihilated in one step. Neither result equals the exact continuous
heat decay. A finite-step stability statement is a different claim from accuracy.

> Why the solve works, and what it costs

For nx>1, the new-time matrix A has diagonal 1+2r and off-diagonals −r.
With D,τ≥0, it is symmetric positive definite and strictly diagonally
dominant. Thomas elimination has positive pivots in exact arithmetic:

\[
p_1=1+2r,\qquad m_i=-r/p_{i-1},\qquad
p_i=1+2r-m_i(-r).
\]
\alttext(The first pivot is one plus twice r; each later multiplier is minus r over the previous pivot, and the new pivot subtracts the multiplier times minus r.)

The matrix is constant throughout a call, so datamunge factors it once, forms
the old-time RHS at each step, then applies forward elimination and backward
substitution. This costs O(nx) factorization work and O(nx) per step. Final-only
storage is O(nx). A complete trace retains the initial profile, every actual
RHS and every subsequent profile, using O(nx(nsteps+1)) storage. It records
executed states directly; it does not rerun prefixes of the simulation.

The trace's scaled residual is a componentwise linear-solve backward error,
max_i |(Au−b)_i|/(|b_i|+(|A||u|)_i), with zero/zero set to zero. A small
value supports the solve of the discrete system, not the accuracy of that
system as an approximation to the continuous PDE.

> A modal stability argument, with the missing qualification

The discrete sine vector v_i=sin(mπi/(nx+1)), 1≤m≤nx, is an eigenvector
of the central-difference heat operator, with

\[
\lambda_{h,m}=-\frac{4D}{h^2}\sin^2\left(\frac{m\pi}{2(nx+1)}\right),
\qquad
g_m=\frac{1+\tau\lambda_{h,m}/2}{1-\tau\lambda_{h,m}/2}.
\]
\alttext(The spatial eigenvalue is minus four D over h squared times a squared sine. The Crank–Nicolson factor is one plus half the time step times that eigenvalue, divided by one minus the same quantity.)

Since λ≤0, |g|≤1 for every τ≥0. The orthogonal sine basis therefore
makes the ideal update nonexpansive in Euclidean norm, hence also in the
uniform-grid discrete L2 norm. This is the unconditional stability statement
for this linear homogeneous problem. It is not an unrestricted claim about
other PDEs, nonlinear solvers, the maximum norm or finite floating arithmetic.

Write z=−τλ/2≥0. Then g=(1−z)/(1+z). When z>1 the mode flips sign,
and g→−1 as z→∞. Crank–Nicolson is A-stable but not L-stable: extremely
stiff modes are barely damped per step. By contrast, exact modal decay
exp(τλ) tends to zero. The one-point control has λ_h=−8 and z=4,
so g=−3/5, exactly the factor observed above.

> Retaining the original sine experiment

Take L=D=1, nx=60, τ=0.001, u0(x)=sin(πx), and inspect times 0,0.05,0.10.
The continuous solution is exp(−π²t)sin(πx). One trace to step 100 supplies
all three profiles. Coordinates, measured norms and actual RHS values are
native results, while heat_sine_mode() supplies independent spectral controls.

The original program read output index 30 but compared against the formula
at index 31. Those points are symmetric for this sine and nearly equal, but
the comparison should still name one coordinate consistently. Here the sample
and all its references use index 30, at x=30/61; the grid has no point at x=1/2.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/crank_nicolson.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const double pi=std::acos(-1.);
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::crank_nicolson_heat_trace([=](double x){return std::sin(pi*x);},1,1,60,.001,100);
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors{{16,185,129},{37,99,235},{124,58,237}};
  std::print("| t | numerical u[30] | continuous u[30] | max PDE error | max ideal-CN error | scaled solve residual |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::size_t color=0;
  for(const int step:{0,50,100}) {
    const auto& state=t.states[step];
    const auto m=dm::algorithms::heat_sine_mode(1,1,60,1,.001,step);
    const auto pde=dm::algorithms::heat_profile_error(state.profile,m.continuous);
    const auto modal=dm::algorithms::heat_profile_error(state.profile,m.discrete);
    if(pde>8e-5 || modal>2e-14) return 1;
    std::print("| {:.2f} | {:.8f} | {:.8f} | {:.6e} | {:.3e} | {:.3e} |\n",state.time,state.profile[30],m.continuous[30],pde,modal,state.scaled_residual);
    plot.line(t.coordinates,state.profile,std::format("t={:.2f}",state.time),colors[color++],2);
  }
  plot.legend({"t=0.00","t=0.05","t=0.10"},colors)
      .title("Original 60-point sine experiment: actual heat profiles")
      .x_label("x").y_label("u(x,t)").x_limits(0,1).y_limits(0,1.05).size(900,560)
      .save_svg("build/crank_nicolson.svg");
  std::print("\nSample coordinate x[30]={:.9f}; one factorization, {} actual solves and {} RHS entries.\n",t.coordinates[30],t.solves,t.rhs_entries);
}
```
