| t | numerical u[30] | continuous u[30] | max PDE error | max ideal-CN error | scaled solve residual |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.00 | 0.99966847 | 0.99966847 | 4.440892e-16 | 4.441e-16 | 0.000e+00 |
| 0.05 | 0.61035975 | 0.61029563 | 6.412286e-05 | 1.477e-14 | 1.292e-16 |
| 0.10 | 0.37266257 | 0.37258427 | 7.829788e-05 | 1.793e-14 | 7.530e-17 |

Sample coordinate x[30]=0.491803279; one factorization, 100 actual solves and 6000 RHS entries.
\image(figures/crank_nicolson.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original three sine profiles retain their small PDE error. Much smaller error against the ideal discrete CN mode checks a different claim: implementation of the discretization.)
\alttext(Three positive sine-shaped curves share zero endpoints, with amplitude near one, zero point six one and zero point three seven at times zero, zero point zero five and zero point one.)

Here τ/h²=3.721, whereas forward Euler's one-dimensional diffusion bound
is Dτ/h²≤1/2. The step is beyond that bound, yet the first sine mode has
positive amplification because its eigenvalue is small compared with the
highest grid mode. This smooth first-mode experiment cannot establish that
all profiles avoid oscillations.

> Separate spatial and temporal errors

For one sine mode, three closed-form amplitudes are available: the continuous
PDE amplitude exp(−D(mπ/L)²t), the exact spatially discretized amplitude
exp(λ_h t), and the ideal CN amplitude g^nsteps. Their differences separate
spatial discretization, time integration and solve rounding.

Expanding the central sine gives λ_h=−D(mπ/L)²+O(h²) for fixed m.
Expanding log(g) for sufficiently small τ gives log(g)=τλ_h+τ³λ_h³/12+O(τ⁵).
Over fixed final time, the global temporal error is O(τ²). Thus, with smooth
compatible data and fixed final time, the combined error is O(h²+τ²), before
rounding dominates. The unnormalized one-step solution defect is O(τ³+τh²);
a residual divided by τ is O(τ²+h²). Calling every version of this quantity
a second-order local error would hide a power of τ.

First refine h while holding τ=0.0001 and final time 0.1. The independent
semidiscrete error isolates space; the actual solver should approach it until
the fixed temporal error matters. Plotting error against h² exposes the
quadratic spatial law without hiding the comparison baseline.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/crank_nicolson_space.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> squared_spacing,errors;
  std::print("| interior nx | h | actual total PDE error | pure spatial modal error | previous/current spatial error |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  double previous=0;
  for(const int nx:{7,15,31,63,127}) {
    const auto m=dm::algorithms::heat_sine_mode(1,1,nx,1,.0001,1000);
    const auto u=dm::algorithms::crank_nicolson_heat([](double x){return std::sin(std::acos(-1.)*x);},1,1,nx,.0001,1000);
    const auto actual=dm::algorithms::heat_profile_error(u,m.continuous);
    const auto space=dm::algorithms::heat_profile_error(m.semidiscrete,m.continuous);
    const auto ratio=previous?previous/space:0.;
    if(previous && (ratio<3.9 || ratio>4.1)) return 1;
    std::print("| {} | {:.6f} | {:.6e} | {:.6e} | {:.6f} |\n",nx,m.grid.dx,actual,space,ratio);
    squared_spacing.push_back(m.grid.dx*m.grid.dx);errors.push_back(actual);previous=space;
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  plot.line(squared_spacing,errors,"actual PDE error",{37,99,235},2)
      .points(squared_spacing,errors,"",{37,99,235},4)
      .title("Spatial refinement: error follows h squared")
      .x_label("h squared, fixed tau=0.0001").y_label("maximum absolute PDE error at t=0.1")
      .x_limits(0,.0165).y_limits(0,.005).size(900,560)
      .save_svg("build/crank_nicolson_space.svg");
}
```
