\image(figures/crank_nicolson_time.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The temporal error approaches a straight line in τ² and the table's halving ratios approach four. The continuous-PDE error is reported separately because it contains spatial error too.)
\alttext(Five observed maximum temporal errors lie almost on a straight line against squared time step, with the finest refinement near the origin.)

> Numeric and resource contracts

The original final-profile signature remains available through pde_solvers.hpp
and the umbrella dm/dm.hpp. Its implementation and the trace use one canonical
kernel. Callbacks are sampled once at each ascending interior coordinate and
never at the two endpoints, which are forced to exact zero. nx must be positive,
nsteps nonnegative, L positive, and D and τ nonnegative; all scalar inputs and
sampled profile values must be finite. D=0 or τ=0 gives identity steps.
Zero steps still validates the grid and returns its initial sampled profile.
Callback exceptions propagate. Invalid dimensions that previously risked empty
Thomas solves or huge allocations now fail explicitly.

Positive spacing and ratio must remain representable, rounded coordinates must
stay strictly inside the domain, and coefficients and final
time must fit finite double storage. Nonfinite stencil/elimination arithmetic
raises std::overflow_error, even though ideal real-arithmetic stability holds.
The widened coefficient calculation and factor reuse can change last-bit
rounding relative to the former implementation; no bitwise identity is promised.

The trace's default cap is 1,000,000 stored profile/RHS scalars. Its exact
count is (nx+2)(nsteps+1)+nx*nsteps; coordinates, factors and metadata are
additional storage. Scalar and derived-coefficient checks precede the complete
budget check, which precedes allocation and callback sampling. An over-budget
call returns no partial trace. A zero cap disables only that cap, and allocation
can still fail. Final-only calls retain O(nx) memory and have no history cap.

No external dataset is needed for these manufactured heat controls. Native
tests additionally compare 500 random small evolutions against an independent
dense long-double Cholesky solver, check actual RHS/factor/step records and norm
contraction, and exercise every sine mode on several grids. They cover both
refinement orders, original profiles, zero-step/zero-diffusion/zero-time cases,
callback order and exceptions, exact and one-below history caps, invalid and
unrepresentable arithmetic, owned storage and profile-error validation. The
focused suite also runs under address and undefined-behavior sanitizers.

> Sources

J. Crank and P. Nicolson (1947), [A practical method for numerical evaluation
of solutions of partial differential equations of the heat-conduction type](https://doi.org/10.1017/S0305004100023197),
Proceedings of the Cambridge Philosophical Society 43(1), 50–67, introduced the
method. The derivations above specialize to this constant-coefficient linear
homogeneous problem. I. Higueras and T. Roldán (2023), [A new insight on positivity
and contractivity of the Crank–Nicolson scheme for the heat equation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2301.01066),
study the separate positivity and maximum-norm questions; this report uses the
explicit one-point counterexample and sine-basis L2 argument rather than claiming
those stronger properties from unconditional stability.
